{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA715652","title":"Mass-immigration determines the assembly of activated sludge microbial communities","description":"The assembly of bacterial communities in wastewater treatment plants (WWTPs) is affected by immigration via wastewater streams, but the impact and extent of bacterial immigrants are still unknown. Here we quantify the effect of immigration at the species-level in eleven Danish full-scale activated sludge (AS) plants. All plants have different source communities but have the same process design, defining the same overall environmental growth conditions. The AS community composition in each plant was strongly reflected by the corresponding influent wastewater (IWW) microbial composition. Most species in AS across the plants were detected and quantified in the corresponding IWW, allowing us to identify their fate in the AS: growing, disappearing, or surviving. Most of the abundant species in IWW disappeared in AS, so their presence in the AS biomass was only due to continuous mass-immigration. In AS, most of the abundant growing species were present in the IWW at very low abundances. We predicted the AS species abundances from their abundance in IWW by using a partial least square regression model. Some species in AS were predicted by their own abundance in IWW, while others by multiple species abundances. Detailed analyses of functional guilds revealed different prediction patterns for different species. We show, in contrast to present understanding, that the AS microbial communities was strongly controlled by the IWW source community and could be quantitatively predicted by taking into account immigration. This highlights a need to revise the way we understand, design, and manage the microbial communities in WWTPs.","organism":null,"data_type":"Raw sequence reads","center_name":"Aalborg University","submission_date":"2021/03/19 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","coun